Now I understand the bug. Looking at `parse_config` in `loader.rs`:

```rust
pub fn parse_config(content: &str, source_path: &Path) -> Result<BivvyConfig> {
    serde_yaml::from_str(content).map_err(|e| BivvyError::ConfigParseError {
        path: source_path.to_path_buf(),
        message: e.to_string(),
    })
}
```

And `BivvyConfig`:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, Serialize, Deserialize)]


#[serde(default)]
pub struct BivvyConfig {
    ...
}
```

The `#[serde(default)]` attribute means that any unknown fields or type mismatches in the YAML get silently ignored, and the struct just uses its default values instead. So if someone has a typo like `ap_name` instead of `app_name`, or misspells `settings`, the parser won't catch it—it'll just create a config with all defaults, which is why the user's configuration changes aren't taking effect.

The solution is to use `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` to make the parser reject any unrecognized keys, which would surface the typos as actual errors instead of silently dropping them. I need to think through where `deny_unknown_fields` would actually take effect. Since deserialization happens after the merge operation in `load_merged_config`, adding this attribute would catch unknown fields at that point for both single-file and merged configurations. For `parse_config`, it would immediately flag any unexpected fields during deserialization. `deny_unknown_fields` on `BivvyConfig` would catch typos at the root level, but nested structs like `Settings` would need it too to catch misspellings in their fields. The most straightforward solution is adding `deny_unknown_fields` to all config structs so typos produce clear parse errors instead of silently falling back to defaults.

Now I need to review the full schema to identify all the structs that need this attribute.

